# s3-23: small computer host handlers (permissions, environment events, legacy command)

No HOST_METHOD rows: the two methods are served (host/computer/permissions, host/computer/command) or sent (computer/userControl)
by name, as host/computer.ts does for host/computer/capture; the host capability `host.computer` they require is already claimed.

1. main/host/index.ts
   a. imports, beside `import { type ComputerControlSource, registerComputerHost } from './computer';`:
        import { type ComputerCommandHostSource, registerComputerCommand } from './computer-command';
        import { type ComputerPermissionReads, registerComputerPermissions } from './computer-permissions';
   b. interface DesktopHostOptions, after the field `computer: ComputerControlSource;`:
        /** The operating-system grants (`host/computer/permissions`): `readComputerPermissions` / `requestComputerPermissions`. */
        computerPermissions: ComputerPermissionReads;
        /** The legacy Windows host's bridge answer while Computer Use is on, else null (`host/computer/command`, dual path). */
        computerCommand: ComputerCommandHostSource;
   c. createDesktopHost, right after `registerComputerHost(connection, options.computer);`:
        registerComputerPermissions(connection, options.computerPermissions);
        registerComputerCommand(connection, options.computerCommand);

2. main/index.ts, in the `createLocalHostRole({ ... })` object, right after its `computer: () => { ... },` entry
   (readComputerPermissions, requestComputerPermissions, assertPublicComputerRequest and ComputerCommand are already imported there):
        computerPermissions: { read: readComputerPermissions, request: requestComputerPermissions },
        computerCommand: () => {
          const host = computerControlEnabled ? computerHost : null;
          if (!host) return null;
          return {
            assertPublic: assertPublicComputerRequest,
            answerBridgeCommand: (command, signal) => host.answerBridgeCommand(command as ComputerCommand, signal),
          };
        },

3. main/browser/test-support/core-harness.ts, in its `createDesktopHost({ ... })` object, after `computer: () => null,`:
        computerPermissions: {
          read: () => ({ screen_capture: 'not_required_on_windows', accessibility: 'not_required_on_windows', input: 'target_integrity_dependent' }),
          request: () => undefined,
        },
        computerCommand: () => null,
   (main/host/host.test.mjs builds its host options in JS: nothing to add, the handlers only register.)

4. main/index.ts
   a. with the other './host/...' imports:
        import { bindComputerEnvironment } from './host/computer-environment';
   b. right after `const localProtocol = localConnection.protocol;` and its null check (powerMonitor and screen are already
      imported from 'electron' there):
        const unbindComputerEnvironment = bindComputerEnvironment(
          powerMonitor,
          screen,
          (method, params) => localProtocol.call(method, params),
          (error) => console.warn('[mixdog-computer] environment takeover report failed:', error)
        );
   c. in `app.on('before-quit', ...)`, beside `desktopTray?.dispose();`:
        unbindComputerEnvironment();
   The old in-process bindComputerEnvironmentGuard call (computer/host/powershell-host.ts) goes with the legacy path (s3-30);
   until then both fire. That is harmless: the core's takeover holds only while a core computer execution is open
   (computer_user_control.rs `hold`: no open execution, no hold), so a report while Computer Use is off changes nothing.
